<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Clive</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1725">1725</a>–<a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Clive of India</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Clive was an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> whose victories in the Carnatic wars checked French power in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. His victory at Plassey in 1757 over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, the nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, made the Company the real power in Bengal, and in 1765 he obtained the diwani of Bengal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-clive.jpg" alt="Robert Clive" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathaniel Dance, (later Sir Nathaniel Dance-Holland, Bt) (died 1811) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Clive,_1st_Baron_Clive_by_Nathaniel_Dance,_(later_Sir_Nathaniel_Dance-Holland,_Bt).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
